items.a:
- [[b:synchronized]]: simple, JVM-optimized (biased/lightweight locking), auto-releases on block exit. But no timeout/interrupt/tryLock, and [[r:causes pinning]] with virtual threads.
- [[b:ReentrantLock]]: flexible — `tryLock`, timeout, interruptible, fairness, multiple Conditions. Must be released in `finally`.
- [[b:StampedLock]]: supports [[g:optimistic reads]] (no locking, validated afterward) — very fast for read-heavy workloads; not reentrant.

<pre><code><span class="c">// 1. synchronized — most concise, but pins the carrier thread of a virtual thread</span>
<span class="k">synchronized</span> (lock) { balance += amount; }
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// 2. ReentrantLock — tryLock/timeout/interruptible; must unlock in finally</span>
<span class="k">if</span> (lock.<span class="f">tryLock</span>(<span class="n">200</span>, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
    <span class="k">try</span> { balance += amount; } <span class="k">finally</span> { lock.<span class="f">unlock</span>(); }
} <span class="k">else</span> {
    <span class="k">throw</span> <span class="k">new</span> BusyException();          <span class="c">// failing fast beats queuing forever</span>
}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// 3. StampedLock — optimistic read: no lock, validate after reading</span>
<span class="k">long</span> stamp = sl.<span class="f">tryOptimisticRead</span>();
<span class="k">long</span> snapshot = balance;
<span class="k">if</span> (!sl.<span class="f">validate</span>(stamp)) {              <span class="c">// a concurrent write happened, so re-read with a real lock</span>
    stamp = sl.<span class="f">readLock</span>();
    <span class="k">try</span> { snapshot = balance; } <span class="k">finally</span> { sl.<span class="f">unlockRead</span>(stamp); }
}</code></pre>

:::deep
- *biased locking* is deprecated (JDK 15) and off by default (18), since it offers little benefit on modern hardware
- the JIT still performs *lock coarsening* (merging adjacent locks) and *lock elision* (dropping locks on non-escaping objects)
- `ReentrantLock` with fair=true is fair but noticeably lowers throughput (FIFO queue)

[[r:StampedLock is NOT reentrant]]; an optimistic read must `validate(stamp)` after reading and must *not* read a reference and then dereference it inside the optimistic block (easy to observe a half-written value).

All three are built on AQS (a CLH queue). With virtual threads, prefer `ReentrantLock` to avoid the pinning caused by `synchronized`.

- Concrete code for all three: `wait`/`notify` vs `Lock`+`Condition` in a bounded blocking queue (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1), and ReadWriteLock/StampedLock/copy-on-write for read-heavy workloads (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4).
:::

- [[b:Checked]]: recoverable errors, must be declared/handled.
- [[b:Unchecked]] (RuntimeException): programming errors or unrecoverable conditions.

The modern trend (Spring): prefer unchecked.
- avoids `throws` spreading everywhere
- wraps infrastructure errors as domain exceptions
- handles them centrally at the boundary (`@ControllerAdvice`)

**Wrapping correctly — keep the `cause`:**
<pre><code><span class="c">// WRONG: swallows the root cause, the real error's stack trace is gone</span>
<span class="k">try</span> {
    gds.<span class="f">issueTicket</span>(pnr);
} <span class="k">catch</span> (IOException e) {
    <span class="k">throw</span> <span class="k">new</span> TicketingException(<span class="s">"issue failed"</span>);   <span class="r">// e is lost</span>
}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// RIGHT: translated into a meaningful business exception but the cause is preserved</span>
<span class="k">try</span> {
    gds.<span class="f">issueTicket</span>(pnr);
} <span class="k">catch</span> (IOException e) {
    <span class="k">throw</span> <span class="k">new</span> TicketingException(<span class="s">"issue failed: "</span> + pnr, e);
}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// TicketingException is checked → by default it does NOT roll back, must be declared explicitly</span>
<span class="k">@Transactional</span>(rollbackFor = TicketingException.<span class="k">class</span>)</code></pre>

:::warn Transaction implication
Spring by default only rolls back on unchecked exceptions. A checked exception [[r:does NOT auto-rollback]] without `rollbackFor`.
:::

:::deep
**At the architectural level:** wrap infrastructure errors (SQLException, IOException) into a meaningful *domain exception* (e.g. `InsufficientBalanceException`) → upper layers stay decoupled from technical detail.
- Spring's `@Repository` automatically translates JPA exceptions into `DataAccessException` (unchecked, categorized)
- handle centrally at the boundary via `@ControllerAdvice`, returning *problem+json* (RFC 7807)
- [[r:never swallow an exception]] (catch, log, and ignore) — fail fast

Remember the rollback rule: only unchecked/Error auto-rolls back; checked exceptions must declare `rollbackFor` — a "money didn't roll back" bug that's very common in fintech.
:::
